From 0764bc49f03a658389842e21924ca4e30a66f16a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:04:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] Record every outbound attempt, log it, and give the log
 a size

_notify_safe() caught every exception from every notification send -- twelve call
sites -- and wrote one line to stderr. The reasoning was right and it stopped
half way: the load *was* invoiced, and an SMTP failure should not turn a
committed write into a 500, but there was no database row, no screen indicator,
no counter and no retry. A broker notification that failed authentication left
Mike looking at a successful Submit and a broker who never heard from him. The
truth vocabulary already had the word for that state; nothing was in a position
to display it because nothing recorded it.

dispatch/delivery.py writes the row BEFORE the send, the only ordering that
survives the failures that matter -- a process killed mid-send, or a transport
that hangs, both leave a QUEUED row the next sweep can see. Backoff is
1/5/15/60/240 minutes then ABANDONED: retrying an auth failure every minute
forever is how an account gets locked, and a queue that never gives up is a queue
nobody reads. A .eml in Archive/Outbox is recorded as SIMULATED, never SENT --
"a file exists" and "a mail server accepted it" are different facts and only one
means the broker heard from Mike.

Nothing resends on a timer. notifications_retry.run_due() is called by the
launcher, a scheduled task or the Maintenance button, so a resend is always
something the operator can point at. It rebuilds each message from the load
rather than from a stored body: a broker receiving, hours later, a description of
a state the load has since left is worse than a message that never arrived.

dispatch/observability.py replaces 80 print() calls and 0 uses of logging with
one key=value line per event, on stderr (so the launcher's server.log capture
keeps working) and in a RotatingFileHandler at 5 MB x 5. The maintenance guide
already said "nothing rotates them automatically"; a log that fills the disk the
database is on takes Dispatch down to record that something went wrong.

Surfaced on /home (the count) and /maintenance (the rows, with the reason).

Also fixes a real race the suite found under load and never in isolation:
/proc/<pid>/cmdline is empty between fork and exec, so control.start() could
record a null command line for the process it will later have to identify before
stop() signals it. Bounded retry, alive-only.
